# DATE(1)

## NAME

date - print and set the date

## SYNOPSIS

| **date** [**-u**] [ yymmddhhmm [ **.**\&ss ] ]

## DESCRIPTION

If no argument is given, the current date and time are printed. If an argument is given, the
current date is set. *yy* is the last two digits of the year; the first *mm* is the month
number; *dd* is the day number in the month; *hh* is the hour number (24 hour system); the
second *mm* is the minute number; **.**\&*ss* is optional and is the seconds. For example:

> date 10080045

sets the date to Oct 8, 12:45 AM. The year, month and day may be omitted, the current values
being the defaults. The system operates in GMT. *Date* takes care of the conversion to and from
local standard and daylight time.

**-u** works in GMT rather than in local time, both when setting and when printing. **Note:**
v7's page documented neither the flag nor the fact that the program has always had it.

## FILES

/usr/adm/wtmp to record time-setting. **Note:** there is no /usr/adm on this system, so the
records are not written; see the BESM-6 notes below.

## SEE ALSO

utmp(5), stime(2), ctime(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

`No permission` if you aren't the super-user and you try to change the date; `bad conversion`
if the date set is syntactically incorrect.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The two-digit year names 1970 through 2069

**This is a deliberate change, and the only one here a user can see.** v7 added 1900 to the two
digits and stopped, so no year after 1999 could be named at all - and **date 0001010000** did
not fail: it meant 1 January 1900, seventy years before the epoch, out of which the day count
came back as a wrong date rather than as an error. Here **70** through **99** mean 1970 through
1999, and **00** through **69** mean 2000 through 2069. The pivot is 70 because that is where a
*time_t* begins on this machine, so every year the window can name is one the clock can hold.

### The machine is always GMT

*TIMEZONE* and *DSTFLAG* in <sys/param.h> are both 0, the kernel's ftime(2) hands those out
unchanged, and there is no zoneinfo to read. So **date** and **date -u** print the same instant
and differ only in where the zone name comes from - which means the DESCRIPTION's "conversion
to and from local standard and daylight time" is arithmetic that runs and adds nothing. It is
kept rather than removed, against the day those two constants are not zero.

### A time_t is one word, so stime(2) takes a value

41 bits of seconds - no 2038 problem - and the gate shed an argument because of it: v7's
*time_t* was two PDP-11 words and its stime(2) took a pointer to both. Here it takes the
number. See stime(2), and intro(2) for why an argument count is a contract between three
places.

### /usr/adm/wtmp does not exist on this image

v7 appends a pair of *utmp* records around a clock change, so that ac(1) can account for the
discontinuity. The code is kept and its write is guarded, as it always was, so nothing happens:
root.manifest declares no /usr/adm, and the directory is deliberately not created - a file that
grew on every clock change would make the image-diffing tests non-reproducible. It comes back
with login(1) and the rest of utmp(5).
